'''Te Tai Tokerau''' ({{lit|The North Coast}}) is a New Zealand parliamentary [[Māori electorates|Māori electorate]] that was created out of the [[Northern Maori]] electorate ahead of the first [[Mixed-member proportional representation|Mixed Member Proportional]] (MMP) election in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]]. It was held first by [[Tau Henare]] representing [[New Zealand First]] for one term, and then [[Dover Samuels]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] for two terms. From [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005]] to 2014, it was held by MP [[Hone Harawira]]. Initially a member of the [[Māori Party]], Harawira resigned from both the party and then Parliament, causing the [[2011 Te Tai Tokerau by-election|2011 by-election]]. He was returned under the [[Mana Party (New Zealand)|Mana Party]] banner in July 2011 and confirmed at the [[2011 New Zealand general election|November 2011 general election]]. In the {{NZ election link|2014}}, he was beaten by Labour's [[Kelvin Davis (politician)|Kelvin Davis]], ending the representation of the Mana Party in Parliament.

== Population centres ==
Te Tai Tokerau's boundaries are similar to those of the pre-[[Mixed-member proportional representation|Mixed Member Proportional]] (MMP) [[Northern Maori]] electorate. Te Tai Tokerau was created ahead of the first MMP [[1996 New Zealand general election|election in 1996]]. In the 2002 boundary redistribution, the size of the electorate shrank to make room for an increase in the number of Māori electorates from six to seven.<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2002 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2002%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=0-478-20169-9 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=2 October 2014 |page=11 |format=PDF |date=21 March 2002}}</ref> The boundaries were not further altered in the 2007 or 2013/14 redistributions.<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=2 October 2014 |page=11 |format=PDF |date=14 September 2007}}</ref><ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=2 October 2014 |page=11 |format=PDF |date=4 April 2014}}</ref>

Te Tai Tokerau is the northernmost [[Māori electorates|Māori electorate]], and covers an area between [[Cape Reinga]] in the [[Far North District|Far North]] of the [[North Island]] to a boundary cutting through West [[Auckland]]. The major population centres are [[Whangārei]], the [[Bay of Islands]] and north and west [[Auckland]]. The electorate contains all of the [[Ngāpuhi]], [[Te Aupōuri]], Ngāti Kuri, [[Te Rarawa]] and [[Ngāti Kahu]] tribal areas, and part of [[Ngāti Whātua]]'s territory (''rohe'').

Its analogous general electorates are {{NZ electorate link|East Coast Bays}}, {{NZ electorate link|North Shore}}, {{NZ electorate link|Northcote}}, {{NZ electorate link|Northland}}, {{NZ electorate link|Rodney}}, {{NZ electorate link|Te Atatū}}, {{NZ electorate link|Upper Harbour}}, {{NZ electorate link|Whangārei}}, most of {{NZ electorate link|Helensville}}, part of {{NZ electorate link|Kelston}} and some of the islands located within {{NZ electorate link|Auckland Central}}.<ref name="Electorate boundaries">{{cite web |title=Electorate Boundaries |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/voters/find-my-electorate |publisher=Elections New Zealand |access-date=6 July 2017}}</ref>

== History ==
[[Image:New Zealand 11 163.jpg|thumb|right|[[Whangārei]]]]

Northern Maori had been held by the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] since the [[1938 New Zealand general election|1938 election]], when longstanding [[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform]] MP [[Taurekareka Henare]] was beaten by Labour's [[Paraire Karaka Paikea]].<ref name="Scholefield 1950">{{cite book |last= Scholefield |first= Guy |title= New Zealand Parliamentary Record, 1840–1949 |author-link=Guy Scholefield |edition=3rd |orig-year= First ed. published 1913 |year= 1950 |publisher=Govt. Printer |location= Wellington |page=131}}</ref> In 1993, after 55 years of his party holding the seat, Labour MP [[Bruce Gregory (politician)|Bruce Gregory]] was beaten by Henare's great-grandson, [[Tau Henare]], standing for [[New Zealand First]], ending Labour's unbroken hold on the four Māori seats. Henare went on to win Te Tai Tokerau after the switch to MMP, and New Zealand First won all five of the newly drawn Māori electorates.

After a tumultuous [[45th New Zealand Parliament|parliamentary term]] which saw all but one of the five New Zealand First Māori MPs defect to other parties, (including Henare himself, who went on to found [[Mauri Pacific]]), Labour won all six Māori electorates contested at the [[1999 New Zealand general election|1999 election]]. In Te Tai Tokerau, Tau Henare was beaten into third place behind the New Zealand First candidate and [[Dover Samuels]], who Henare had beaten three years previous.

However, Labour's losing the five Māori electorates in 1996 showed that the Māori vote was contestable for the first time in five decades, as the new electoral system coupled with the rise of small parties meant that non-Labour candidacy in these seats was more feasible than under [[First Past the Post]].

The [[New Zealand foreshore and seabed controversy]] of 2004–05 proved to be the catalyst for the second challenge to Labour party domination of the Māori electorates, this time from the [[Māori Party]]. At the [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005 election]], Samuels and three other Labour Māori MPs lost their seats to Māori Party challengers. In Te Tai Tokerau, the winner was [[Hone Harawira]].<ref name="Harawira parliamentary bio">{{cite web|title=Hone Harawira |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/MPs/MPs/0/f/a/50MP126921-Harawira-Hone.htm |publisher=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=3 December 2011}}</ref><ref name="Te Tai Tokerau 2005 election">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – Te Tai Tokerau|url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2005/electorate-67.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Office, Wellington |access-date=3 December 2011}}</ref>

Harawira resigned from the Māori Party in early 2011 and became an independent MP. On 11 May 2011, he resigned from Parliament effective 20 May, seeking a mandate for his new party, the [[Mana Party (New Zealand)|Mana Party]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10724918 |title=Harawira resigns from Parliament |date=11 May 2011 |access-date=11 May 2011}}</ref><ref>http://www.elections.org.nz/voting/te-tai-tokerau-by-election/te-tai-tokerau-by-election-results.html {{dead link|date=December 2016}}</ref> This caused the 25 June 2011 [[2011 Te Tai Tokerau by-election|by-election]], which was contested by five parties, with the main contenders Harawira, [[Kelvin Davis (politician)|Kelvin Davis]] (Labour Party) and Solomon Tipene (Māori Party). Harawira retained the electorate with a majority of 1,117,<ref name="Te Tai Tokerau by-election 2011 results">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – Te Tai Tokerau |url= http://electionresults.govt.nz/2011_te_tai_tokerau_byelection/ |publisher=New Zealand Electoral Commission |access-date=3 December 2011}}</ref> his previous majority being over 6,000.<ref name="Te Tai Tokerau 2008 election">{{cite web|title=Official Count Results – Te Tai Tokerau |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2008/electorate-68.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Office, Wellington |access-date=3 December 2011}}</ref> In the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 general election]] some months later, Harawira had a similar majority to Davis.<ref name="2014 election">{{cite web| url= http://electionresults.org.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-69.html |title=Official Count Results – Te Tai Tokerau |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |date=20 September 2014|access-date=20 April 2015}}</ref>

The Mana Party formed a [[coalition]] with the [[Internet Party (New Zealand)|Internet Party]] just prior to the [[2014 New Zealand general election]]. The coalition was registered with the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] as the [[Internet Party and Mana Movement]] in July 2014, allowing it to contest the party vote.<ref name="Internet MANA registration">{{cite web |title=Registration of Internet Party and MANA Movement logo |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/registration-internet-party-and-mana-movement-logo |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |date=24 July 2014 |access-date=8 August 2014}}</ref> The Internet Party was founded by controversial online millionaire [[Kim Dotcom]], and this strategic coalition resulted in Harawira's main opponent, Labour's Kelvin Davis, getting endorsements from [[Winston Peters]] of [[New Zealand First]]<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bennett |first1=Adam |title=Election 2014: Winston Peters hits out at National after big poll surge |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11328639 |access-date=3 October 2014 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=21 September 2014}}</ref> and the Prime Minister, [[John Key]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].<ref>{{cite news|last1=McQuillan|first1=Laura|title=Key's subtle endorsement for Kelvin Davis|url=http://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/auckland/election/news/83283426-key-s-subtle-endorsement-for-kelvin-davis|access-date=3 October 2014|work=[[Newstalk ZB]]|date=17 September 2014}}</ref> Even the electorate's candidate for the [[Māori Party]], Te Hira Paenga, reminded voters of the importance of strategic voting.<ref>{{cite news |title=Davis picking up endorsements |url= http://www.waateanews.com/waateanews/x_story_id/NzkxNg==/Davis-picking-up-endorsements |access-date=3 October 2014 |work=Radio Waatea |date=19 September 2014}}</ref> In his fourth challenge in the Te Tai Tokerau electorate, Davis ousted the incumbent Harawira, which ended the representation of the Mana Party in Parliament.<ref name="Ousted Harawira">{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Simon |title=Davis' win a critical blow for Harawira, Internet Mana |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/10524510/Davis-win-a-critical-blow-for-Harawira-Internet-Mana |access-date=30 September 2014 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=20 September 2014}}</ref>

